<p><a name="footnote1"></a><sup>1</sup> I  would like to thank James Egelhof and Antoine Martin for their assistance in  the preparation of these remarks, Adam Biesenbach for his assistance with the  calculation of the dispersion indices presented here, Logan Suba for his  assistance with data, and colleagues in the Federal Reserve System for numerous  insightful comments and suggestions.</p>
<p><a name="footnote2"></a><sup>2</sup> See, for example: Bernanke, &ldquo;<a href="https://www.federalreserve.gov/boarddocs/speeches/2005/20050330/">Implementing  Monetary Policy</a>,&rdquo; March 30, 2005; Ihrig, Meade and Weinbach &ldquo;<a href="https://www.aeaweb.org/articles?id=10.1257/jep.29.4.177">Rewriting  Monetary Policy 101: What's the Fed's Preferred Post-Crisis Approach to Raising  Interest Rates</a>?, &rdquo; <em>Journal of  Economic Perspectives</em>, Fall 2015. </p>
<p><a name="footnote3"></a><sup>3</sup> Conceptually, the federal funds rate is a measure of what banks pay to take out  unsecured overnight loans from other banks, government-sponsored entities, and  certain other entities.  The technical  definition of a federal funds transaction is provided in Regulation D, <a href="https://www.ecfr.gov/cgi-bin/text-idx?SID=a2f4ce85b9eb9212d8496df0471c41f3&mc=true&node=se12.2.204_12&rgn=div8">12  CFR 204.2(</a>a)(1)(vii).  The effective  federal funds rate is calculated as the volume-weighted median of federal funds  transactions reported by banks on FR 2420, Report of Selected Money Market  Rates.  See &ldquo;<a href="https://www.newyorkfed.org/markets/opolicy/operating_policy_150708.html">Statement  Regarding the Calculation Methodology for the Effective Federal Funds Rate and  Overnight Bank Funding Rate</a>,&rdquo; July 8, 2015.</p>
<p><a name="footnote4"></a><sup>4</sup> The FOMC&rsquo;s September 2014 <a href="https://www.federalreserve.gov/newsevents/pressreleases/monetary20140917c.htm">Policy  Normalization Principles and Plans</a> indicate that the Committee expects to  reduce the Federal Reserve&rsquo;s balance sheet in a gradual and predictable manner,  primarily by ceasing to reinvest repayments of principal.  On March 15, 2017, Chair Yellen stated:  &ldquo;We&rsquo;ve emphasized for quite some time that the Committee wishes to use variations  in the fed funds rate target – our short-term interest rate target – as our key  active tool of policy.&rdquo;  She also stated  that while &ldquo;asset purchases are a tool we could conceivably resort to if we  found ourselves in a serious downturn where we were, again, up against the zero  lower bound and faced with substantial weakness in the economy, it&rsquo;s not a tool  we would want to use as a routine tool of policy.&rdquo;  See the transcript of <a href="https://www.federalreserve.gov/mediacenter/files/FOMCpresconf20170315.pdf">Chair  Yellen&rsquo;s March 15, 2017 press conference</a>.</p>
<p><a name="footnote5"></a><sup>5</sup> Overnight RRP operations supplement IOR by offering reverse repurchase  agreements at a specified offering rate to a broad set of counterparties,  including several types of nonbank financial institutions that are significant  lenders in U.S. money markets.  See  Potter, &ldquo;<a href="https://www.newyorkfed.org/newsevents/speeches/2015/pot150415.html">Money  Markets and Monetary Policy Normalization</a>,&rdquo; April 15, 2015, and Frost,  Logan, Martin, McCabe, Natalucci and Remache, &ldquo;<a href="https://www.federalreserve.gov/econresdata/feds/2015/files/2015010pap.pdf">Overnight  RRP Operations as a Monetary Policy Tool: Some Design Considerations</a>,&rdquo;  February 19, 2015.</p>
<p><a name="footnote6"></a><sup>6</sup> Designated financial market utilities may be paid interest on balances maintained at the Federal Reserve as well, as provided for in the Dodd-Frank Act and Regulation HH.  The payment of interest to these entities does not appear to have had a measurable impact on money markets to date.</p>
<p><a name="footnote7"></a><sup>7</sup> Recall that federal funds loans are unsecured.   Like the vast majority of central banks, the Federal Reserve conducts  its open market operations in the secured market.</p>
<p><a name="footnote8"></a><sup>8</sup> Although households and firms do not directly transact in the federal funds  market, the effective federal funds rate is used as a reference rate in the  setting of a variety of private sector contracts, including in interest rate  futures and swaps.  Many bank loans are  priced to <em>The Wall Street Journal</em>&rsquo;s  Prime Rate, which since the mid-1990s has been set by convention to 300 basis  points above the FOMC&rsquo;s target rate or range.</p>
<p><a name="footnote9"></a><sup>9</sup> See Dudley, &ldquo;<a href="https://www.newyorkfed.org/newsevents/speeches/2017/dud170330">The  Importance of Financial Conditions in the Conduct of Monetary Policy</a>,&rdquo;  March 30, 2017.</p>
<p><a name="footnote10"></a><sup>10</sup> When I refer to repo rates, I mean those on repo backed by Treasury securities  generally, rather than specific Treasury issues – that is, &ldquo;general collateral&rdquo;  repo..  See Copeland, Davis, LeSueur and  Martin, &ldquo;<a href="http://libertystreeteconomics.newyorkfed.org/2012/06/mapping-and-sizing-the-us-repo-market.html">Mapping  and Sizing the U.S. Repo Market</a>,&rdquo; June 25, 2012.</p>
<p><a name="footnote11"></a><sup>11</sup> These  new floating net asset value, liquidity fee and gating rules apply to municipal  (tax-exempt) money market funds as well.   Municipal (tax-exempt) money markets are beyond the scope of my remarks.</p>
<p><a name="footnote12"></a><sup>12</sup> Competition could have suffered, for example, if too few prime funds continued  lending to banks, giving the remaining funds some market power.  Similarly, if the supply of funds in the  secured market were to become overly large relative to banks&rsquo; balance sheet  capacity, those banks willing to accept these funds could potentially obtain  some market power.</p>
<p><a name="footnote13"></a><sup>13</sup> Other money market investors have similar broad mandates and play a role in  promoting relative value between the secured and unsecured markets.  However, some such investors, such as nonfinancial  corporations and foreign official institutions, appear to have limited ability  to dynamically reallocate funds among asset classes, perhaps because of limited  information or investment mandate restrictions.   Further research to explore mechanisms by which money market lenders  substitute between secured and unsecured investments may prove helpful.</p>
<p><a name="footnote14"></a><sup>14</sup> Chen, Cipriani, La Spada, Mulder and Shah. &ldquo;<a href="http://libertystreeteconomics.newyorkfed.org/2017/03/money-market-funds-and-the-new-sec-regulation.html">Money  Market Funds and the New SEC Regulation</a>,&rdquo; March 20, 2017.</p>
<p><a name="footnote15"></a><sup>15</sup> For a broad perspective on how financial regulation changed after the crisis, see Tarullo, “<a href="https://www.federalreserve.gov/newsevents/speech/tarullo20170404a.htm">Departing Remarks</a>,” April 4, 2017.</p>
<p><a name="footnote16"></a><sup>16</sup> The U.S. regulatory capital rules include two leverage ratios: the tier 1  leverage ratio and the supplementary leverage ratio (SLR). The tier 1 leverage  ratio applies to all banking organizations subject to the regulatory capital  rules.  It is measured as tier 1 capital  divided by weekly or daily average total consolidated assets.  The minimum tier 1 leverage ratio requirement  is 4 percent and is currently in effect. The SLR is effective January 1, 2018  with public disclosure requirement in effect starting in January 1, 2015, and  will apply only to &ldquo;advanced approaches banking organizations&rdquo; That are defined  as those with at least $250 billion in total consolidated assets or at least  $10 billion in consolidated on-balance sheet foreign exposures. It will be  measured as tier 1 capital divided by total leverage exposure, which equals the  daily average of total consolidated assets plus month-end average of certain  off-balance-sheet exposures. The minimum SLR requirement will be 3 percent. In  addition, there will be an enhanced SLR requirement applicable to U.S. top-tier  bank holding companies identified as globally systemically important banking  organizations (G-SIBs). The enhanced requirement consists of a 2 percent  leverage buffer above the minimum SLR requirement, for a total of 5 percent.</p>
<p><a name="footnote17"></a><sup>17</sup> Burhouse, Feid, French and Ligon, &ldquo;<a href="https://www.fdic.gov/bank/analytical/fyi/2003/011403fyi.html">Basel and  the Evolution of Capital Regulation: Moving Forward, Looking Back</a>,&rdquo; January  14, 2003.</p>
<p><a name="footnote18"></a><sup>18</sup> Other  financial regulations, such as the G-SIB surcharge and the FDIC assessment fee,  have similar economic effects. One factor that helps determine the surcharge  level is based on a bank&rsquo;s systemic footprint.</p>
<p><a name="footnote19"></a><sup>19</sup> Suppose  a securities dealer borrows $100 and lends $100 in repo.  If a 5 percent leverage ratio requirement  were binding on this dealer, and that dealer planned its business around a 10  percent return on equity, it would require a 50 basis point interest rate  spread to cover its capital cost.  Such a  cost would be quite substantial relative to typical money market spreads.  Of course, this is a stylized example, and  banks could well choose to apply a lower cost of capital than used here for  low-risk activities like money market intermediation.  The mechanism by which banks determine their  cost of capital for individual activities remains a topic of active research  without a strong consensus.  See Stiroh,  &ldquo;<a href="https://www.newyorkfed.org/newsevents/speeches/2017/sti170223">A  Perspective on Supervisory Objectives and Trade-offs</a>,&rdquo; February 23, 2017.</p>
<p><a name="footnote20"></a><sup>20</sup> See also Duffie, &ldquo;<a href="http://www.risk.net/cutting-edge/views/2479855/why-leverage-ratio-distorts-market-making">Why  the leverage ratio distorts market-making</a>.&rdquo; Risk, January 3, 2017.</p>
<p><a name="footnote21"></a><sup>21</sup>  There are likely other disincentives to  dealers&rsquo; acting as intermediaries in money markets.  For example, the Dodd-Frank Act&rsquo;s prohibition  on proprietary trading may limit banks&rsquo; ability or willingness to engage in activity  that is classified for regulatory purposes as arbitrage, such as in the foreign  exchange swaps market.  </p>
<p><a name="footnote22"></a><sup>22</sup> This discussion is intended as a high-level summary, and omits a number of  other technical requirements for transactions to be eligible for netting.  The financial accounting rules for balance  sheet netting for repo transactions are spelled out in Financial Accounting  Standards Board (FASB) Interpretation No. 41, &ldquo;<a href="http://www.fasb.org/jsp/FASB/Document_C/DocumentPage?cid=1175801626916&acceptedDisclaimer=true">Offsetting  of Amounts Related to Certain Repurchase and Reverse Repurchase Agreements</a>&rdquo;  (FIN 41).  For other derivatives  contracts, FASB Interpretation No. 39, &ldquo;<a href="http://www.fasb.org/cs/BlobServer?blobcol=urldata&blobtable=MungoBlobs&blobkey=id&blobwhere=1175820930240&blobheader=application%2Fpdf">Offsetting  of Amounts Related to Certain Contracts</a>&rdquo; (FIN 39) states the relevant  requirements.</p>
<p><a name="footnote23"></a><sup>23</sup> I  discussed the foreign repo pool in my 2016 remarks.  See Potter, &ldquo;<a href="https://www.newyorkfed.org/newsevents/speeches/2016/pot160222">Money  Markets after Liftoff: Assessment to Date and the Road Ahead</a>,&rdquo; February 22,  2016.  It is also discussed in the annual  report.</p>
<p><a name="footnote24"></a><sup>24</sup> See &ldquo;The Recent Increase in the Effective Fed Funds and Overnight Bank Funding  Rates,&rdquo; Wrightson ICAP, October 17, 2016.</p>
<p><a name="footnote25"></a><sup>25</sup> See &ldquo;Factors Affecting Reserve Balances,&rdquo; <a href="https://www.federalreserve.gov/releases/h41/20160310/">March 10, 2016</a> and <a href="https://www.federalreserve.gov/releases/h41/20170105/default.htm">January  5, 2017</a>.</p>
<p><a name="footnote26"></a><sup>26</sup> Duffie and Krishnamurthy, &ldquo;<a href="https://www.kansascityfed.org/~/media/files/publicat/sympos/2016/econsymposium-duffie-krishnamurthy-paper.pdf?la=en&amp;hash=B2998C204B3401F908FE03FCE3014180">Passthrough  Efficiency in the Fed&rsquo;s New Monetary Policy Setting</a>,&rdquo; 2016.</p>
<p><a name="footnote27"></a><sup>27</sup> In  constructing this time series, Darrell and Arvind combine two things: before  mid-2009, they use a now-discontinued secondary market rate for tradable  certificates of deposit.  This is a  wholesale market rate, and during normal times it generally tracked the federal  funds rate.  However, after mid-2009,  they use a primary market deposit rate (i.e., rates offered in bank branches to  customers) that did not rise after the December 2015 tightening.  This low responsiveness of primary market  rates to tightening of monetary policy is consistent with similar behavior  during previous tightening cycles, as documented by Driscoll and Judson, &ldquo;<a href="https://www.federalreserve.gov/pubs/feds/2013/201380/201380pap.pdf">Sticky  Deposit Rates</a>,&rdquo; October 1, 2013.<br><br>
      The OECD&rsquo;s dataset includes a 90-day interest rate series.  Pre-2013, it similarly used the  now-discontinued secondary-market CD rate data.   However, post-2013 it uses the interest rate on 90-day AA-rated  financial commercial paper.  In contrast  to the retail rate Darrell and Arvind use, this commercial paper rate rose  along with the federal funds rate in late 2015.  </p>
<p><a name="footnote28"></a><sup>28</sup> In  the unsecured market, we also sometimes see small upticks ahead of holiday  weekends, such as on days that are not Federal banking holidays but on which  the Securities Industry and Financial Markets Association has recommended closure  or early closure.  For example, we saw  modest, transitory upticks in dispersion on March 24, 2016 (Good Friday) and  December 23, 2016 (the business day before the Christmas holiday).  A similar uptick was observed on November 23,  2016 (the day before Thanksgiving).</p>
<p><a name="footnote29"></a><sup>29</sup> In  the measure that combines both markets, we see dispersion fall on month- and  quarter-end dates; this occurs because rates tend to fall in the unsecured  market on these dates, pushing them closer together.</p>
<p><a name="footnote30"></a><sup>30</sup> I  discussed these factors in my February 2016 speech.</p>
<p><a name="footnote31"></a><sup>31</sup> Some government-sponsored entities can also participate in the dealer-to-dealer  market.</p>
<p><a name="footnote32"></a><sup>32</sup> This is due to covered interest rate parity: the proposition that the interest  rate differential between two currencies of a given tenor should equal the  differential between the spot exchange rate and the future exchange rate of the  same tenor.  See Borio, McCauley, McGuire  and Sushko, &ldquo;<a href="http://www.bis.org/publ/qtrpdf/r_qt1609e.pdf">Covered  interest parity lost: understanding the cross-currency basis</a>,&rdquo; September  2016.</p>
<p><a name="footnote33"></a><sup>33</sup> Counterparty risk may have risen somewhat since the crisis.  However, in the absence of acute financial  stress, the impact of counterparty risk on interest rates paid by  well-capitalized financial firms is likely to be quite modest for overnight or  short-term tenors.</p>
<p><a name="footnote34"></a><sup>34</sup> In other jurisdictions there is also evidence that a restricted supply of good  collateral is exacerbating the impact of these factors.</p>
<p><a name="footnote35"></a><sup>35</sup> A  number of other factors have likely been relevant at the margin.  For example, monetary policy divergence  across currency areas, and shortages of high-quality foreign collateral, may  have put pressure on FX swap markets.   See Nakaso, &ldquo;<a href="http://www.boj.or.jp/en/announcements/press/koen_2017/data/ko170120a1.pdf">Monetary  Policy Divergence and Global Financial Stability: From the Perspective of  Demand and Supply of Safe Assets</a>,&rdquo; January 20, 2017.</p>
<p><a name="footnote36"></a><sup>36</sup> Much  net borrowing in interdealer repo comes from smaller or less creditworthy  dealers, and a large portion of foreign exchange swap borrowing market appears  to come from firms that have large dollar asset holdings and copious  foreign-currency financing, but that lack a U.S. deposit base or direct  wholesale dollar money market presence. To the extent that these firms had  previously had access to financing from money market funds, in many cases this  came from prime funds, which as I mentioned earlier have shrunk  considerably.  <br>
      <br>
      The apparently short-run inelastic demand for funds from these firms has  supported higher intermediation spreads in these markets, particularly on  period-end dates.  Over time, these  higher spreads might encourage these firms to seek more stable sources of  dollar funding or decrease their dollar assets, either of which would likely be  positive for financial stability.   Indeed, we have heard anecdotally that firms of this type are  increasingly shifting towards wholesale deposits from nonfinancial corporations.  </p>
<p><a name="footnote37"></a><sup>37</sup> For  example, if repo rates fell relative to other rates, we might see more take-up  at our ON RRP operations, or if foreign exchange swap rates were to rise  further, we might see greater use of the official dollar auctions conducted by  foreign central banks that are part of the standing swap network.  </p>
<p><a name="footnote38"></a><sup>38</sup> CGFS Paper No. 54, &ldquo;<a href="http://www.bis.org/publ/cgfs54.pdf">Regulatory  change and monetary policy</a>,&rdquo; May 2015.</p>

    </div>
         

</div>

</div>
